{"challenges":[{"attack":"Test the excluded case x=2, where cancellation would divide by zero.","claim_id":"factorization","outcome":"survives","result":"The polynomial identity still holds at x=2: both sides equal 0; it does not assert quotient cancellation." ,"tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Check whether any real input besides 2 makes the denominator zero.","claim_id":"original-domain-claim","outcome":"survives","result":"x−2=0 if and only if x=2, while the numerator is a polynomial defined for every real x.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Check that cancellation is performed only after establishing x−2≠0.","claim_id":"restricted-simplification","outcome":"survives","result":"restricted-cancellation explicitly assumes x≠2, so division by the nonzero factor x−2 is valid.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Compare the two expressions at x=2 and distinguish equality on a common restricted domain from equality as total real-valued functions.","claim_id":"not-global-equivalence","outcome":"survives","result":"x+2 has value 4 at x=2, whereas the original quotient is undefined there; thus they are not equivalent at every real input, although they agree for exactly x≠2.","tool_ids":[]}],"checks":[{"basis_reasoning":"factor-identity directly expands the right-hand side and obtains x²−4, establishing the universal polynomial identity without any division.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["factor-identity"],"claim_id":"factorization","reasoning":"The claimed factorization follows by direct expansion for arbitrary real x.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"denominator-condition identifies the sole zero of x−2, and original-domain-step checks both inclusion for x≠2 and exclusion at x=2.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["denominator-condition","original-domain-step"],"claim_id":"original-domain-claim","reasoning":"The numerator is defined for all reals and the denominator is nonzero exactly when x≠2.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"restricted-cancellation depends on factor-identity and denominator-condition and cancels only the explicitly nonzero factor x−2.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["factor-identity","denominator-condition","restricted-cancellation"],"claim_id":"restricted-simplification","reasoning":"For every real x≠2, substitution of the factorization followed by valid cancellation yields x+2.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"compare-at-two establishes the different status at x=2, while agreement-domain combines it with restricted-cancellation to establish equality precisely on the original domain.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["original-domain-step","restricted-cancellation","compare-at-two","agreement-domain"],"claim_id":"not-global-equivalence","reasoning":"The functions agree at all and only inputs where the quotient is defined; at x=2 the simplified polynomial has value 4 but the quotient has no value.","verdict":"supported"}],"missing_evidence":[],"recommended_action":"finish","tool_requests":[]}
